00:10:00,060 --> 00:10:07,380
And this is a really important distinction because now whenever you want to do parallel work with Cloud Code, you have to make that decision.

116
00:10:07,470 --> 00:10:12,612
Should I ask Cloud to use sub-agents or should I ask Cloud to spin up an agent team?

117
00:10:13,006 --> 00:10:25,320
And spoiler, there still are a lot of times where you want to use subagents instead, especially because of a couple of problems with agent teams that I'll talk about that'll lead really nicely into this skill that I have built for you.

118
00:10:25,460 --> 00:10:29,020
This makes Clawd a lot better at using agent teams.

119
00:10:29,100 --> 00:10:30,780
So I'm really excited to show you this.

120
00:10:31,060 --> 00:10:32,964
But first, let's talk about subagents.

121
00:10:33,454 --> 00:10:36,900
So the primary idea with subagents is context isolation.

122
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:45,420
We want some way to be able to dish out a request that could take tens or even hundreds to the thousands of tokens, but all we need back is a summary.

123
00:10:45,680 --> 00:10:52,900
So our primary agent knows generally what happened, but it doesn't have to be polluted by the context of the entire task.

124
00:10:53,300 --> 00:10:59,460
And this is important because context is the most precious resource when you are using an AI coding assistant.

125
00:10:59,820 --> 00:11:13,120
But this context isolation has downsides to it because there is no coordination between sub-agents and the entire process is just a black box because it is only the summary, the final output that is given back to our primary agent.

126
00:11:13,660 --> 00:11:24,612
And so that is why I say that sub-agents are generally used for focused tasks, usually something like research, because all we care about is the result, that summary at the end of the research.

127
00:11:24,942 --> 00:11:33,360
If we're doing something like coding and we have a sub-agent actually write code, then we don't have any idea into the process of the sub-agent.

128
00:11:33,440 --> 00:11:40,580
And so the main agent loses a lot of context as to what was actually implemented, which is why I say research over implementation.

129
00:11:40,740 --> 00:11:42,160
There's no coordination at all.

130
00:11:42,446 --> 00:11:54,212
These sub-agents work completely in isolation, which does make them very token efficient because they're honed in on a single task and they're only communicating a little bit back to the primary agent.

131
00:11:54,360 --> 00:11:56,500
But sometimes you need a lot more than that.

132
00:11:56,640 --> 00:12:01,400
Sometimes you need your agents to coordinate with each other, manage a task list together.

133
00:12:01,520 --> 00:12:03,588
And that is where Agent Teams comes in.

134
00:12:04,380 --> 00:12:08,484
With Agent Teams, we still have a primary agent and spinning off these sub-processes.

135
00:12:09,080 --> 00:12:12,020
But the difference here is they're actually talking to each other.

136
00:12:12,160 --> 00:12:17,920
So they have this shared task list, they're updating each other on their progress, communicating to the main agent as well.

137
00:12:18,222 --> 00:12:23,260
And you can instruct Cloud Code in a lot of different ways how this communication actually takes place.

138
00:12:23,566 --> 00:12:25,280
We'll talk about that in a little bit.

139
00:12:25,646 --> 00:12:28,020
So we have true peer-to-peer coordination.

140
00:12:28,660 --> 00:12:30,916
And this is so powerful for implementation.

141
00:12:31,566 --> 00:12:43,340
Because for example, our backend agent might change something in an API endpoint where we would have to tell the front-end agent, hey, I changed this API, make sure you update the front-end component that uses the API as well.

142
00:12:43,598 --> 00:12:45,160
And it can actually do that.

143
00:12:45,230 --> 00:12:51,960
When we had sub-agents in the past doing that kind of implementation, those kinds of things would break all the time because they're not talking to each other.

144
00:12:52,046 --> 00:12:55,260
So you'd step on each other's toes, but not know they're doing so.

145
00:12:55,580 --> 00:13:00,964
And so it was up to the main agent after to find all those bugs and fix it, and it was just a complete mess.

146
00:13:01,440 --> 00:13:04,004
And so Agent Teams is a lot better for implementation.

147
00:13:04,040 --> 00:13:10,080
But, you have to keep in mind that sub-agents are a lot more token efficient.

148
00:13:10,300 --> 00:13:20,900
It takes a lot of tokens to set up this task list, maintain that collaboration and the communication between the lead agent and all of the other agents in the team.

149
00:13:21,340 --> 00:13:31,748
And so this is a really, really rough estimate, but yeah, oftentimes when you're using agent teams, it's like two to four times the token usage compared to just using Cloud Code by itself or using sub-agents.

150
00:13:32,334 --> 00:13:35,380
And so you oftentimes need the collaboration for coding.

151
00:13:35,790 --> 00:13:45,660
So generally I would say, if you want a really simple rule of thumb right now, you should use sub-agents for any kind of research, like diving into a code base or searching the web.

152
00:13:46,030 --> 00:13:49,400
And then you should use agent teams for your actual implementation.

153
00:13:49,960 --> 00:14:03,920
And so a lot of times when you're working with a single conversation of cloud code, you might start with sub-agent research, like analyzing the code base, and then create that task list and spin up the agent team to knock out the plan that you created from the research.

154
00:14:04,140 --> 00:14:10,340
So again, it's sub-agents for research, feed that into a plan, and then send that plan into an agent team.

155
